('82d1e6ed-8cb3-5168-bd97-32cd4d4542b7'::uuid,'eafa0947-d859-57e8-b93e-affd8500f0a5'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.6)',E'**CHAPTER 4**\n\n**EARLY WARNING, ACTIVATION, INCIDENT MANAGEMENT  \nAND EMERGENCY RESPONSE**\n\nORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)  \nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft  \nSubject to institutional, operational, technical and legal validation*\n\n**Draft 1.1 \\| 8 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Document attribute** | **Control information**                                                                                                                                                            |\n|------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document title         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 4                                                                                                                             |\n| Chapter title          | Early Warning, Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response                                                                                                              |\n| Document status        | Working draft for technical, institutional and legal validation                                                                                                                    |\n| Document owner         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                                                 |\n| Technical custodian    | CDRRMO Head / designated Operations and Warning focal person                                                                                                                       |\n| Reviewing offices      | City Legal Office; OCD Region VIII; PAGASA; PHIVOLCS; MGB; City Health Office; CSWDO; City Engineering; PNP; BFP; Coast Guard and other competent response agencies, as applicable |\n| Version / date         | Draft 1.1 / 8 November 2026                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Approval authority     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements                                                                                                         |\n| Review cycle           | At least annually; after a significant emergency, exercise, material legal or technical change, or identified operational deficiency                                               |\n\n## REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date**   | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | **Prepared / reviewed by**        | **Approval** |\n|-------------|------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | 8 Nov 2026 | Initial assessment-informed working draft; aligned with Chapters 1–3 and current MODE repository architecture. Indicator-level IAT v1.5 workbook scores/action rows require final reconciliation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Project drafting team             | For review   |\n| 1.1         | 8 Nov 2026 | IAT rows A6, A7, A8 and B1.2–B1.5 reconciled with the controlled IAT v1.6 and MR workbooks (Annex 4-A); every SOP step states its actor, decision, inputs, outputs, timing, control and escalation; SOP control blocks; sources supplied on 8 Nov 2026 incorporated (DILG VIII reporting SOP and alert-status triggers, CP Guidebook 2020, JMC No. 2 s. 2021, Gawad KALASAG CY 2026 toolkit, CDRRMO structure chart); five figures; APA captions; Annexes 4-A to 4-D. | Project drafting team with CDRRMO | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nEarly warning and emergency response are not isolated communication or rescue activities. They are a coordinated chain that connects authoritative hazard monitoring, impact interpretation, risk communication, local decision-making, preparedness actions, incident command, EOC coordination, response services and transition to recovery. The effectiveness of this chain depends on whether information reaches the right decision-maker in time, whether decisions are made by the legally authorised person or body, and whether actions are carried out through clearly assigned institutions and documented procedures.\n\nChapters 1–3 establish the institutional, workforce and risk-intelligence foundations for this chapter. Chapter 1 defines the mandates and decision rights of the City Mayor, CDRRMC, CDRRMO, EOC and Incident Command System (ICS). Chapter 2 establishes the permanent CDRRMO workforce and the personnel arrangements that support an activated EOC. Chapter 3 establishes the risk-information base, scenario and contingency planning, inclusive preparedness and continuity arrangements. Chapter 4 translates those foundations into an operational system for warning, readiness, activation, incident coordination and response support.\n\nThe October 2026 institutional assessment and the Chapter 1–3 drafts indicate that the City has operational practices and important response capabilities, but that the institutionalisation of the warning-to-action chain requires further documentation, approval, exercise and evidence. The CDRRMO''s normal duty and monitoring functions, the developing 24-hour OpCen, scenario-specific preparedness and the proposed EOC structure must function as one coherent system without confusing permanent office management, EOC coordination and field command.\n\nThis chapter applies systems thinking and Total Quality Management (TQM). It considers warning and response as an end-to-end process with feedback loops: warning receipt and verification; impact and exposure analysis; decision and authorisation; dissemination and acknowledgement; protective action; operational monitoring; review; and improvement. A warning successfully transmitted is not necessarily a warning understood or acted upon. An activation order is not evidence of adequate staffing. A response activity is not complete until its outcome, outstanding needs and handover are recorded.\n\n| VALIDATION NOTE. Draft 1.1 reconciles the indicator references with the controlled IAT workbook (v1.6, 8 November 2026) and the Master Requirements Traceability and 140-Indicator Data Dictionary (v1.1). Annex 4-A copies each A6, A7, A8 and B1.2–B1.5 row (score, priority, compliance, recommended action, owner, target, status and evidence-validation status) without change. All 29 rows are still "Pending validation" in the workbook, so the scores remain provisional until the December 2026 validation workshop. Chapters 1 and 2 (Draft 1.2, 6 November 2026) and Chapter 3 (v1.0, 8 November 2026) are the companion texts. |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\n## 1.1 Operational Context and Priority Design Considerations\n\n**Table 4.1**\n\n*Operational Context and Priority Design Considerations*\n\n| **Design consideration**               | **Existing context / evidence in Chapters 1–3 and repository**                                                                                                                                                                                    | **Chapter 4 response**                                                                                                                                 |\n|----------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Permanent monitoring and activated EOC | October 2026 site observation recorded 24-hour duty for City Traffic Monitoring, Public Safety and Emergency Monitoring at the CDRRM Office/OpCen. Chapter 1 proposes the OpCen as the operating base that transitions into an EOC on activation. | Separate routine monitoring and duty-officer functions from an activated EOC. Document the transition, escalation, staffing and authority.             |\n| White / Blue / Red operating levels    | Chapter 1 proposes White normal operations, Blue partial activation and Red full activation; the application implements these level names and state transitions.                                                                                  | Define level purpose, readiness posture, approval, notification, staffing and deactivation controls; scenario plans specify technical triggers.        |\n| Scenario-based EOC configuration       | Chapter 1 proposes one EOC structure with scenario-specific modules; Chapter 2 proposes pre-designated augmentee and barangay liaison arrangements. ADR-017 defines EOC structure and cluster planning.                                           | Use a common organisational structure; activate only the functions and cluster desks required by the validated scenario.                               |\n| ICS–EOC relationship                   | Chapter 1 identifies undefined ICS–EOC interface/chain of command as a high-priority institutional gap (B6.1, score 1 in the chapter narrative).                                                                                                  | State explicitly that Incident Commander/IMT command field operations; EOC coordinates support, information, resources and executive decision support. |\n| Warning-to-action procedures           | Chapter 1 records that pre-emptive evacuation and work/class suspension have been applied event by event; Chapter 3 requires scenario-specific triggers and plans.                                                                                | Create a controlled warning-action matrix with source, threshold, approving authority, action owner, target groups and acknowledgement evidence.       |\n| EOC legal/operational establishment    | Chapter 1 records no EOC-establishing EO at assessment time; proposed January 2027 action is to establish the City EOC by local issuance.                                                                                                         | Make EOC establishment and approved activation authority a prerequisite for adoption; the MOO itself does not create legal authority.                  |\n| Response service interfaces            | The City relies on CDRRMO, City offices, barangays, national agencies, utilities and response clusters with distinct mandates.                                                                                                                    | Document activation and coordination interfaces without transferring sectoral, regulatory or field-command mandates.                                   |\n| Digital support and records            | MODE implements EOC activation levels, append-only logbook, SitReps, barangay counts, resource requests, role-based access and local-first sync.                                                                                                  | Use approved records and system controls consistently; distinguish locally captured drafts from server-confirmed authority actions.                    |\n\n## 1.2 Operational System and Control Logic\n\nThe City''s early warning and emergency response system shall follow the sequence below. The steps may overlap during fast-onset events, but the responsible actors and decision rights shall remain clear.\n\n**Table 4.2**\n\n*Stages of the City Early Warning and Response System*\n\n| **Stage**       | **Core activity**                                                                                        | **Primary accountable function**                                                       | **Required evidence**                                                                         |\n|-----------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Monitor     | Receive official bulletins, advisories, observations and community reports.                              | CDRRMO Operations and Warning / designated monitoring duty officer                     | Source, issue time, receipt time, hazard, location and verification status.                   |\n| 2\\. Interpret   | Assess forecast, likely impacts, exposure, vulnerability, lead time and scenario assumptions.            | CDRRMO technical coordination with source and sector offices                           | Impact note / PDRA or technical assessment, assumptions and uncertainty.                      |\n| 3\\. Decide      | Select operational posture and protective action; obtain required approval.                              | Authority identified in law, ordinance, EO and approved contingency plan               | Decision record with authority, rationale, time, scope and conditions.                        |\n| 4\\. Disseminate | Transmit warnings and instructions through agreed channels; seek receipt and understanding.              | Designated public information / warning focal person with barangays and sector offices | Message version, issue time, sender, channel, audience, acknowledgements and failed delivery. |\n| 5\\. Act         | Implement monitoring, prepositioning, evacuation, service protection, rescue and other approved actions. | Relevant lead agency / barangay / Incident Commander according to assignment           | Tasking, deployment, status, safety check and completion evidence.                            |\n| 6\\. Coordinate  | Consolidate situation, unmet needs, requests, constraints and decision requirements.                     | Activated EOC Manager and EOC sections                                                 | Common operating picture, decision log, resource status and SitRep.                           |\n| 7\\. Transition  | Scale down, transfer unresolved tasks and shift to recovery/continuity arrangements.                     | Mayor/CDRRMC, CDRRMO Head, EOC Manager and responsible offices                         | Deactivation/transition decision, handover, outstanding action register and review plan.      |\n| 8\\. Learn       | Review performance and revise plans, warning arrangements and training.                                  | CDRRMO with responsible offices and CDRRMC oversight                                   | After-action review, corrective action and controlled revision.                               |\n\n**Figure 4.1**\n\n*The End-to-End Warning-to-Action Chain*\n\n![Figure 1: 1.2 Operational System and Control Logic](/library/ch4v11/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Stages 3 and 4 (shaded) are where most failures occur: an action without an identified authority, or a message transmitted but not received. Feedback from learning (stage 8) revises the plans, the matrix and the training. Prepared for this chapter from Table 4.2.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter establishes the City''s minimum operating arrangements for receiving and verifying early warnings, translating risk information into timely action, activating the EOC, coordinating incident support and documenting response performance. It does not create an independent command system or transfer the legal powers of the Mayor, CDRRMC, Sangguniang Panlungsod, technical agencies or Incident Commander.\n\nThe intended institutional results are:\n\n- An authoritative, traceable warning-receipt and verification process linked to approved hazard-specific contingency plans.\n\n- A locally approved warning-to-action matrix with clear decision authority, lead/support offices, target groups, dissemination channels and acknowledgement requirements.\n\n- A documented White–Blue–Red activation and deactivation process, including the transition from the existing OpCen duty arrangement to an activated EOC.\n\n- A scenario-based EOC structure that scales according to incident complexity while retaining a stable reporting architecture and clear relationship with ICS/IMT.\n\n- Operational arrangements for incident objectives, operational periods, tasking, coordination meetings, escalation and handover, consistent with the applicable ICS doctrine and local issuances.\n\n- Coordinated and accessible warning and protective-action arrangements for barangays and at-risk groups, including persons with disabilities, older persons, children, pregnant/lactating women and other groups identified in the risk assessment.\n\n- Documented response coordination for evacuation, search and rescue, emergency medical and public health support, protection, shelter/CCCM, WASH, logistics, utilities and other sector functions according to their mandates.\n\n- Consistent incident records, logbook entries, SitReps, decision records, barangay reports and resource status information, with privacy and data-integrity controls.\n\n- Exercise-tested procedures, after-action reviews and corrective actions that demonstrate institutional use rather than documentary completion alone.\n\n# 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\nThis chapter applies to CDRRMO warning, monitoring, duty-officer and response-coordination functions; the City EOC when activated; barangay warning and response arrangements; and the designated City offices, response agencies, utilities and partners that contribute to emergency operations. It covers normal monitoring, heightened preparedness, anticipatory action, emergency response, operational transition and deactivation.\n\n**Table 4.3**\n\n*Institutional Authority Retained and Chapter 4 Interfaces*\n\n| **Institution / actor**                        | **Authority and accountability retained**                                                          | **Operational interface under Chapter 4**                                                                                                                                 |\n|------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| City Mayor / Local Chief Executive             | Executive authority and decisions assigned by law and valid City issuances.                        | Approves or directs measures within lawful authority; receives escalated decisions; issues activation-related executive directions where provided by the approved EOC EO. |\n| CDRRMC                                         | Statutory policy, coordination, oversight and recommendations under RA 10121 and applicable law.   | Provides policy direction, reviews readiness and response priorities, recommends actions/declarations where legally required and monitors performance.                    |\n| CDRRMO Head                                    | Permanent office leadership, technical coordination and office accountability.                     | Ensures duty and warning capability, technical advice, EOC readiness, coordination and post-event review; does not replace field command.                                 |\n| Operations and Warning Division / duty officer | Routine monitoring, warning coordination and operational readiness within approved office mandate. | Receives and logs warning information, verifies sources, escalates risk, maintains contact directories and supports early action and activation.                          |\n| Activated EOC Manager                          | EOC operational coordination authority under approved local issuance and activation.               | Manages EOC operations, assigns EOC staff, coordinates sections and cluster desks, consolidates information and elevates decisions.                                       |\n| Incident Commander / IMT                       | Incident-level command, objectives and tactical direction under the applicable ICS arrangement.    | Directs field operations; requests coordination and resources through the EOC; provides situation updates and receives support.                                           |\n| Response clusters / lead agencies              | Sectoral and statutory mandates, professional standards and operational command in their fields.   | Lead and coordinate their assigned response services; designate liaison; report outputs, needs and constraints to EOC.                                                    |\n| Barangays / BDRRMCs                            | Barangay DRRM governance, local warning, evacuation and community-level action.                    | Receive and relay warnings; implement approved barangay actions; report status and unmet needs; maintain community feedback.                                              |\n| Technical warning agencies                     | Authority for their official forecasts, advisories, bulletins and technical interpretation.        | Provide source products and technical guidance; retain authority over the technical meaning and validity of their products.                                               |\n| Utilities / private and humanitarian partners  | Own assets, service delivery, contractual and organisational responsibilities.                     | Provide agreed continuity, status, mutual aid, technical support and resources through designated liaison channels.                                                       |\n\nThe chapter uses the functions of the JMC 2014-1 model recommended in Chapter 2 (Administration and Training, Research and Planning, Operations and Warning). The CDRRMO structure chart in use in 2026 groups the same work into four sections. Table 4.4 shows where each Chapter 4 function sits today, so that procedures can be assigned now and carried over unchanged when the recommended structure is adopted.\n\n**Table 4.4**\n\n*Chapter 4 Functions in the Current CDRRMO Structure (2026)*\n\n| **Chapter 4 function (recommended model)**          | **Current section (2026 structure chart)** | **Positions involved (no names)**                                                                                                   | **Chapter 4 procedures**               |\n|-----------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| Monitoring, warning receipt, OpCen duty             | Warning and Monitoring Section             | Section head (designate); Operations Center Team Leader; Operations Center staff and radio operators                                | SOP 4-01, 4-02; SOP HR-05 handover     |\n| Response teams, rescue and training                 | Operations and Training Section            | Local DRRM Officer; Operations Team Leader (24/7 medical emergency rescue team); technical rescuers; rescue drivers; training staff | SOP 4-05, 4-06 (field support); ER-F02 |\n| Risk information, PDRA inputs, contingency planning | Research and Planning Section              | Local DRRM Officer; utility and liaison staff                                                                                       | SOP 4-03; Chapter 3 SOP 3-6            |\n| Records, reports, procurement, liaison              | Administrative Section                     | Section head (designate); records, reports, receiving, procurement and liaison staff                                                | SOP 4-07, 4-08 (records and archive)   |\n\n*Note.* Prepared from the CDRRMO organizational structure chart (2026), which lists positions in each section; Chapter 2 (Draft 1.2) proposes the move to the JMC 2014-1 divisions. Where the chart shows a vacant or designate post, the procedure names the function, not the post.\n\n| VALIDATION NOTE. The EOC is an operational coordination mechanism. It does not replace the permanent CDRRMO, does not become a fourth CDRRMO division, and does not command field response. The Incident Commander/IMT retains incident-level command under the approved ICS structure. Solid reporting relationships and dotted coordination/support links in the EOC structure must be shown distinctly. |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\nThe provisions of this chapter shall be read with the legal and policy hierarchy established in Chapter 1. The table below identifies principal anchors; the City Legal Office and OCD Region VIII shall verify current texts, applicability and local approval routes before adoption.\n\n**Table 4.5**\n\n*Principal Legal, Policy and Operational Instruments*\n\n| **Instrument / guidance**                                                                                                                                            | **Operational relevance**                                                                                                                                                                                             | **Application / control**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA No. 10121 (2010) and its IRR                                                                                                                                      | Local DRRM system, local councils and offices, early warning, contingency planning, preparedness, response and recovery.                                                                                              | CDRRMO and CDRRMC functions shall be performed within statutory mandates and applicable IRR provisions.                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| RA No. 7160 (1991), Local Government Code                                                                                                                            | Local executive authority, general welfare and local government powers.                                                                                                                                               | Local decisions, orders and service actions shall be approved by the official or body with the relevant legal authority.                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| RA No. 12287 (2025) and its 2026 IRR                                                                                                                                 | State of Imminent Disaster and anticipatory action.                                                                                                                                                                   | Use the separate Chapter 1 / GOV-05 process; do not conflate an imminent-disaster declaration with EOC activation or State of Calamity.                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012                                                                                                                            | Institutionalisation and use of ICS.                                                                                                                                                                                  | Apply the current official ICS doctrine and approved City ICS issuance; validate the EOC–ICS interface with OCD Region VIII.                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018: Guidelines on the Establishment, Operationalization and Management of Emergency Operations Center (title as in the project copy) | Guidelines for establishment, operationalisation and management of EOCs.                                                                                                                                              | Obtain the certified/official copy and validate title, provisions, activation guidance and applicability with OCD Region VIII.                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| PAGASA / PHIVOLCS / MGB official products and advisories                                                                                                             | Authoritative technical information for hydrometeorological and geohazard conditions.                                                                                                                                 | Preserve original source, issue time, validity, location and technical wording. CDRRMO does not alter official warning thresholds.                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| DILG VIII Memorandum R08-LGCDD-2025-02-26-008 (26 February 2025) adopting RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006, s. 2023: Disaster Response Reporting Template and SOP   | Pre-disaster, during, post-disaster and terminal reports; conditions and triggers for raising the alert status by hazard (Table 2); reporting frequency by alert status (Table 3); data checklist by phase (Table 4). | The LGU EOC uses the harmonized template from the raising of Blue. Alert levels are raised by the RDRRMC or by the LGU through an EO within its area, on the recommendation of its LDRRMC (section IV.3). Cut-offs: section 11.2 and Figure 4.5. Confirm with DILG VIII that this remains the current version. |\n| RA No. 10173 and applicable NPC issuances                                                                                                                            | Personal and sensitive personal information in incident, evacuation and beneficiary data.                                                                                                                             | Apply purpose limitation, minimisation, role-based access, secure sharing, retention and breach controls.                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| RA Nos. 9710, 7277 as amended, 9994, 10821, 8371, 11036 and 11106                                                                                                    | Gender-responsive, disability-inclusive, older-person, child, IP, mental health and Filipino Sign Language considerations.                                                                                            | Warning formats, evacuation, shelter, health/protection services and feedback shall be accessible and context-sensitive.                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| RA No. 12076 and its IRR                                                                                                                                             | Standards for permanent evacuation centres.                                                                                                                                                                           | Evacuation decisions and facility use must consider approved facility suitability, access, capacity and safety; not every public building is an evacuation centre.                                                                                                                                             |\n| City-approved EOC EO, ICS EO, contingency plans and relevant ordinances                                                                                              | Local organisation, activation authority, alert levels, tasking and reporting.                                                                                                                                        | No proposed arrangement in this chapter takes effect until the relevant local issuances and plans are approved and aligned.                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| NDRRMC-OCD Contingency Planning Guidebook (February 2020)                                                                                                            | Contingency plan structure (Background, Goal and Objectives, Response Arrangements, Activation) and CP Forms 1–11, including EOC and ICS forms.                                                                       | Activation follows the plan flow: PDRA, RDANA or intelligence reports as triggers; the Incident Commander recommends termination. Used in SOP 4-03, 4-04, 4-09 and Chapter 3 SOP 3-6.                                                                                                                          |\n| DSWD-DILG-DOH-DepEd Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2, s. 2021: Guidelines on Camp Coordination and Camp Management and IDP Protection (supersedes JMC No. 1, s. 2013) | Camp Management Committee organised by the LGU through its LDRRMC, with the local social welfare officer as overall coordinator; camp management plan; DAFAC registration; IDP protection.                            | SOP 4-06 and form ER-F03. DAFAC and family data stay with the CSWDO; EOC records carry counts only.                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| OCD Gawad KALASAG LDRRMO Assessment Toolkit, CY 2026                                                                                                                 | Means of verification for a functional 24/7 operations center, alternate EOC, early warning system, alert-status orders and ICS.                                                                                      | Annex 4-D maps each Chapter 4 record to the toolkit evidence, so that operating records double as assessment evidence.                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| NEDA Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide                                                                                                             | Post-disaster recovery planning.                                                                                                                                                                                      | SOP 4-09 hands recovery tasks to Chapter 8, which applies the guide.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n\n| VALIDATION NOTE. NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018 is held in the project files as a scanned copy titled Guidelines on the Establishment, Operationalization and Management of Emergency Operations Center; its operative provisions must still be checked against a certified copy. Legal readings from scanned documents should remain provisional until verified by OCD Region VIII and the City Legal Office. Do not encode unverified technical thresholds or legal deadlines as fixed operating rules. |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\n# 5. Systems-Thinking and TQM Principles for Early Warning and Response\n\n**Table 4.6**\n\n*Systems-Thinking and TQM Principles Applied to Warning and Response*\n\n| **Principle**                   | **Required application**                                                                                | **Quality control**                                                                                                                             |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| End-to-end warning chain        | Link source monitoring, impact analysis, decision, dissemination, receipt, action and feedback.         | Maintain time stamps for every stage; identify delay, failed delivery and no-acknowledgement conditions.                                        |\n| Authoritative source provenance | Use original products from competent technical agencies and verified local observations.                | Record issuing agency, product title/number, issue and expiry times, geographic scope, version and access source.                               |\n| Risk-informed and impact-based  | Interpret the hazard together with exposure, vulnerability, critical services and access constraints.   | Document assumptions, uncertainty, at-risk locations and population groups; do not translate forecast alone into an unapproved action.          |\n| Decision rights                 | Every operational action has an identified legal/administrative authority and implementing lead.        | Decision record names decision-maker, basis, time, geographic scope and conditions.                                                             |\n| Inclusive communication         | Warnings are understandable, actionable, timely and available through appropriate channels and formats. | Test language, visual/audio options, relay arrangements and acknowledgement with target communities.                                            |\n| Scalable coordination           | EOC structure and staffing expand according to incident complexity and approved activation.             | Activation checklist, staffing roster, alternates, liaison roster and section handover.                                                         |\n| ICS interoperability            | EOC support complements incident objectives and field command.                                          | ICS/EOC briefing, resource request traceability, command/coordination boundary and escalation log.                                              |\n| PDCA / TQM                      | Plan procedures, implement actions, check performance and correct weaknesses.                           | Exercises, after-action review, corrective action owner, due date and verification record.                                                      |\n| Data protection and integrity   | Capture only information needed for operational decisions and legally authorised reporting.             | No names of evacuees, patients or informants in general-purpose logbook or public map layers; restrict detailed lists to authorised custodians. |\n\n# 6. Early Warning and Risk Communication Architecture\n\n## 6.1 Warning Sources and Verification\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a source register for official hazard monitoring and warning products. The technical source agency remains responsible for the accuracy, interpretation and update of its product. The CDRRMO shall record and relay the product faithfully, add local impact information only when its source and validation status are clear, and request clarification where technical products appear inconsistent.\n\n**Table 4.7**\n\n*Warning Sources and Minimum Recorded Fields*\n\n| **Information type**                                                              | **Primary source / custodian**                                                       | **Minimum recorded fields**                                                                                                                    | **Control**                                                                                                                |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Tropical cyclone, rainfall, flood and climate information                         | PAGASA; relevant City engineering, drainage and river monitoring sources             | Product title/number, issuing office, issue/validity time, warning area, forecast period, rainfall/wind information and update reference.      | Do not invent rainfall, wind or flood thresholds. Use the exact official category and local thresholds only where adopted. |\n| Earthquake, ground shaking, fault, liquefaction, tsunami and volcanic information | DOST-PHIVOLCS                                                                        | Bulletin/advisory number, origin time, magnitude/intensity as stated, affected area, tsunami information, recommended actions and update time. | Do not reinterpret magnitude/intensity into a local evacuation order without the approved technical and decision process.  |\n| Rain-induced landslide and geohazard information                                  | DENR-MGB and competent technical sources                                             | Map/report title, product date, susceptibility class, coverage, limitations and field validation status.                                       | Susceptibility maps are not parcel-level engineering determinations.                                                       |\n| Incident and community reports                                                    | Barangays, responders, utilities, hospitals, field teams and verified channels       | Reporter institution/role, location, time observed/received, content, verification status and follow-up.                                       | Do not treat an unverified social-media report as an official warning or confirmed incident.                               |\n| Critical service and facility status                                              | Asset-owning City offices, hospitals, utilities, port/operator and relevant agencies | Asset/service ID, affected location, status, service users, dependency, estimated disruption and source.                                       | The asset owner validates technical status and restoration estimates.                                                      |\n\n## 6.2 Warning Receipt, Verification and Escalation\n\n1.  Receive the official product or report through an approved source channel. Record the issue time shown by the source and the time received by the City duty officer. Preserve the original product or a traceable reference to it.\n\n2.  Check issuing agency, product identifier, authenticity, validity period, geographic coverage, hazard type and update/cancellation status. Where the source is unclear or a product appears superseded, contact the issuing agency or its designated channel.\n\n3.  Enter the information in the warning/monitoring log and identify the likely affected barangays, exposed population, critical facilities, lifelines and essential services using the current approved risk-information base.\n\n4.  Compare the product with the relevant approved contingency plan and warning-action matrix. Identify the actions that may be initiated by the duty officer, the actions requiring EOC Manager/CDRRMO Head recommendation and the actions requiring Mayor/CDRRMC or other lawful authority.\n\n5.  Escalate material changes, imminent impacts, conflicting information, suspected failure of a lifeline or a report of casualties/large displacement through the duty roster and approved escalation directory. Do not delay urgent life-safety notifications while routine records are being completed.\n\n6.  Issue an internal warning brief to the CDRRMO Head and relevant decision-makers. State the source, issue/receipt times, hazard, affected area, forecast/observation, expected impacts, lead time, confidence/uncertainty, recommended options and decision required.\n\n7.  Update the log when a new bulletin supersedes or cancels a prior product. Notify recipients of material changes and record which earlier message/version is superseded.\n\n## 6.3 Warning-to-Action Matrix\n\nThe City shall maintain a controlled matrix for each priority hazard in its approved contingency plans. This chapter provides the mandatory fields and decision logic; the actual numerical thresholds and action triggers must be copied from authoritative agency products and adopted local plans or issuances. No trigger is created by this draft.\n\n**Table 4.8**\n\n*Required Fields of the Hazard-Specific Warning-to-Action Matrix*\n\n| **Matrix field**             | **Required content**                                                                                                                                         |\n|------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Hazard / scenario            | Hazard type, scenario ID, affected geographic area and plan version.                                                                                         |\n| Authoritative trigger        | Exact warning/advisory category, bulletin condition or verified observation; issuing agency and source reference.                                            |\n| Lead time / validity         | Issue time, forecast/observation window, expiry, update cadence and uncertainty.                                                                             |\n| Decision authority           | Specific official or body authorised to approve each action under law and local issuance.                                                                    |\n| Recommended decision options | Monitor; heighten readiness; partial/full activation; issue public advisory; prepare or recommend evacuation; deploy sector teams; request external support. |\n| Implementing lead / support  | Responsible office/agency, barangay, response cluster, utility or partner.                                                                                   |\n| Target audience              | Affected barangays, households/communities, commuters, schools, facilities, responders and other groups.                                                     |\n| Communication channels       | Approved official channels, redundant channels, relay arrangements and accessible formats.                                                                   |\n| Receipt and feedback         | Acknowledgement method, non-receipt escalation, clarification/rumour process and community feedback channel.                                                 |\n| Evidence and review          | Decision record, message copy, dissemination log, action status, outcome and plan revision reference.                                                        |\n\n| VALIDATION NOTE. The matrix shall not establish technical warning thresholds by this Manual alone. Each hazard-specific threshold must be cited to a current official source or valid City-approved contingency plan. The competent authority must approve the corresponding local action. Drought, heat, health, industrial/geothermal and other slower-onset or technological scenarios require their own validated decision pathways; they must not be forced into a typhoon/flood trigger template. |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\nThe regional reference for the matrix is Table 2 of the DILG VIII reporting SOP (RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006, s. 2023), which lists the conditions and triggers for raising the alert status by hazard. Annex 4-C summarises it. Those triggers become City triggers only when the approved EOC Executive Order or a hazard-specific contingency plan adopts them; until then the matrix cites them as the regional reference.\n\n## 6.4 Risk Communication and Warning Dissemination\n\nThe designated warning/public information focal person shall coordinate dissemination in accordance with approved City communication and approval arrangements. Technical warnings must retain their official meaning. Any City message that translates a warning into local protective action must identify the authorised issuer, action, affected area, effective time, practical instruction, support information and the channel for clarification.\n\n- Use more than one communication channel where practicable, with backup arrangements for power, mobile network or internet disruption.\n\n- Use clear, concise and action-oriented language. Avoid unexplained technical terms and ambiguous expressions such as “prepare” where a specific action is intended.\n\n- Provide appropriate formats and relay support for people with hearing, visual, cognitive, mobility or language barriers. Coordinate with barangay officials, CSOs and relevant sector offices for local accessibility and comprehension.\n\n- Identify the official source and time of the warning and state whether the information is preliminary, updated, cancelled or superseded.\n\n- Distinguish an advisory, recommendation, evacuation instruction and legally authorised order. Do not imply that a CDRRMO or EOC communication has authority it does not possess.\n\n- Record failed delivery and non-acknowledgement. Escalate to alternate channels and local relays. A sent message alone is not evidence that the intended audience received and understood it.\n\n# 7. Operational Readiness, Heightened Preparedness and Anticipatory Action\n\n## 7.1 Routine Monitoring and Duty-Officer Readiness\n\nDuring normal operations, the CDRRMO shall maintain the routine monitoring and duty-officer arrangement established under Chapter 2. The duty officer shall follow the approved roster, handover and escalation procedures; monitor official sources; maintain the contact directory; record significant events and warnings; and ensure that current contingency plans, checklists and communication tools are accessible.\n\n**Table 4.9**\n\n*Routine Monitoring and Duty-Officer Readiness Controls*\n\n| **Readiness element** | **Minimum control / evidence**                                                                               | **Owner**                                            |\n|-----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| Duty coverage         | Approved roster, named primary and alternate, contact method, shift handover and absence cover.              | Operations and Warning Division / CDRRMO Head        |\n| Source monitoring     | Current source directory, access links, alert subscriptions and source-verification checklist.               | Duty officer / warning focal person                  |\n| Communications        | Tested primary and alternate channels, equipment condition, charging/power and escalation contacts.          | Operations and Warning; ICT/communications custodian |\n| Scenario readiness    | Current contingency plans, warning-action matrices, barangay contact directory and facility/resource status. | CDRRMO with responsible sector offices and barangays |\n| Readiness reporting   | Recorded significant developments, unresolved issues, resource gaps and recommended posture.                 | Duty officer to CDRRMO Head / EOC Manager on call    |\n| Continuity            | Backup arrangements for power, communications, records, alternate work location and key-person cover.        | CDRRMO Head and continuity-designated offices        |\n\n## 7.2 Heightened Preparedness and Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment\n\nWhen an official warning, verified observation or other approved trigger indicates increasing risk, the duty officer shall notify the CDRRMO Head and initiate the heightened-preparedness workflow in the relevant contingency plan. The CDRRMO shall coordinate a Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment (PDRA) or equivalent technical assessment where required by the applicable plan and national guidance. Technical sector offices and barangays shall provide current impact, exposure, vulnerability, capacity and resource information within their mandates.\n\n8.  Open a preparedness record and identify the hazard scenario, source products, geographic coverage, forecast/observation period and time of next review.\n\n9.  Convene the relevant technical offices, barangays and partner agencies through the approved coordination arrangement. Confirm participants, roles and decision deadlines.\n\n10. Compile available information on people and assets at risk, vulnerable groups, access constraints, critical facility/lifeline dependencies, evacuation capacity, response resources and service continuity.\n\n11. Identify uncertainty, missing data and assumptions. Distinguish modelled estimates, reported observations and field-verified facts.\n\n12. Develop options and recommendations: monitor; preposition resources; prepare transport and evacuation support; protect critical facilities; issue public advisories; request heightened staffing; recommend partial or full EOC activation; or refer matters for executive/CDRRMC decision.\n\n13. Record decisions, approving authority, responsible office, completion time and unresolved gaps. Reassess when a material warning or observation changes.\n\n| VALIDATION NOTE. Anticipatory-action declaration and funding steps under RA 12287 are governed by Chapter 1 GOV-05 and the applicable IRR. Chapter 4 covers operational coordination and readiness inputs; it does not independently determine legal declaration eligibility, substitute for the prescribed RDRRMC process, or authorise LDRRMF use. |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\n## 7.3 Prepositioning and Readiness Actions\n\nPrepositioning shall be risk-informed, documented and undertaken by the responsible office under its approved authority, procurement, property and financial procedures. The EOC may coordinate status and priorities when activated or when authorised to do so, but it shall not bypass the procurement, accounting, property or expenditure controls established in Chapter 5 and applicable law.\n\n- Confirm the responsible asset owner, location, quantity, condition, availability, transport requirement and deployment authority.\n\n- Check accessibility, handling, security, environmental and safety requirements for supplies and equipment.\n\n- Record issue, transfer, custody, receiving officer and replenishment arrangements.\n\n- Coordinate resource placement with the relevant barangays, evacuation facilities, response teams and utility operators.\n\n- Update the readiness picture and identify resources that are committed, unavailable, damaged, in transit or awaiting replenishment.\n\n# 8. EOC Activation, Scaling and Deactivation\n\n## 8.1 Purpose and Activation Principles\n\nThe EOC provides the City''s central mechanism for operational coordination during emergencies and heightened incidents, as established by the valid local EOC issuance. Activation brings together the functions and personnel needed to maintain a shared operational picture, support incident command, coordinate response clusters, prioritise resource requests and elevate decisions. Activation does not itself confer statutory powers or change the employment and administrative relationships of designated staff.\n\n- Activation shall follow approved triggers, authority, notification and documentation in the EOC EO and contingency plans.\n\n- The EOC is scalable. Only functions and cluster desks required by the scenario and incident complexity are staffed, while the common organisational structure remains recognisable.\n\n- The EOC Manager manages EOC coordination. The Incident Commander/IMT directs on-scene operations.\n\n- The CDRRMO continues its permanent office functions while supporting the activated EOC.\n\n- If the EOC facility is unsafe or unavailable, the approved alternate arrangement shall be used. Do not invent an alternate EOC site in the Manual.\n\n## 8.2 White, Blue and Red Operating Levels\n\n**Table 4.10**\n\n*White, Blue and Red Operating Levels*\n\n| **Level**                 | **Operating posture**                                                                                                      | **Illustrative functions / staffing posture**                                                                                          | **Control and evidence**                                                                                  |\n|---------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| White – Normal            | Routine monitoring, readiness, planning and duty-officer operation.                                                        | Existing OpCen/duty team; CDRRMO Head or designated EOC Manager on call, subject to approved roster.                                   | Daily monitoring and duty log; readiness checks; contact and source update.                               |\n| Blue – Partial activation | Heightened preparedness or incident coordination needs additional management, planning, information or logistics capacity. | EOC Manager and selected EOC functions; scenario-relevant section/unit leads and liaison focal persons on standby or activated.        | Recorded trigger/reason, approving authority, staffing notification, activated functions and review time. |\n| Red – Full activation     | Incident scale, complexity or coordination demands require full EOC functions and relevant cluster/liaison desks.          | Full EOC sections, command staff and required cluster/liaison functions; shifts and alternates in accordance with the approved roster. | Activation decision, role assignments, shift plan, briefing, operational objectives and decision log.     |\n\n| VALIDATION NOTE. The White/Blue/Red descriptions are consistent with the platform and Chapter 1 proposal. Numerical thresholds, minimum headcount, staffing numbers and reporting cut-offs must be confirmed against the approved EOC EO, official NDRRMC EOC guidance, the Chapter 2 staffing proposal and current DILG Region VIII reporting instructions. The proposed “about 50 persons over two shifts” and the provincial reporting cut-offs shown in Chapter 1 remain validation items until confirmed by source records. |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n\nUnder section IV.3 of the DILG VIII reporting SOP, warning agencies recommend the raising of EOC alert levels; the recommendation goes to the RDRRMC for approval or as agreed in the PDRA, and an LGU may raise its own level through an Executive Order within its area on the recommendation of its LDRRMC. The City EOC Executive Order should name the official who signs that order and the CDRRMC route for the recommendation (SOP 4-04).\n\n## 8.3 Activation Workflow\n\n14. Initiate. A warning, incident report, verified field observation or contingency-plan trigger is received and logged by the duty officer or responsible official.\n\n15. Assess. The CDRRMO Head or designated officer reviews the hazard/scenario, expected impacts, current level, capacity, requests and applicable approved trigger.\n\n16. Recommend. The CDRRMO Head or designated official recommends the activation posture and functions required, consistent with the EOC EO and contingency plan.\n\n17. Authorise. The official designated in the approved EOC EO makes the activation decision. If the issuance provides for separate approval routes by level or scenario, follow that route.\n\n18. Notify. The designated activation officer issues the activation notice to the EOC Manager, section leads, rostered staff, cluster/liaison desks and partner agencies; records successful and failed notifications.\n\n19. Configure. The EOC Manager opens the operational record, assigns activated sections, confirms alternates and liaison desks, checks facility/communications readiness and establishes briefing and reporting arrangements.\n\n20. Brief. The EOC Manager conducts an initial briefing covering situation, objectives, known impacts, assumptions, safety, current authority, operational period, reporting, resource priorities and unresolved decisions.\n\n21. Operate and review. The EOC Manager reviews level and staffing as conditions change, records reasons for scaling up/down and seeks approval where required.\n\nFigure 4.2 shows how the activation steps connect. It follows the sample contingency-plan activation flow of the CP Guidebook (2020), in which activating the plan places the EOC on Red and convenes the clusters, while a decision not to activate keeps the EOC on Blue with responders on normal ICS operations; the Incident Commander recommends termination.\n\n**Figure 4.2**\n\n*EOC Activation and Deactivation Flow*\n\n![Figure 2: 8.3 Activation Workflow](/library/ch4v11/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Adapted from the sample activation flow chart in the NDRRMC-OCD Contingency Planning Guidebook (2020, p. 24) and section IV.3 of the DILG VIII reporting SOP (2025). A State of Imminent Disaster follows the separate route of SOP GOV-05. The flow shows sequence and authority only; triggers come from the approved EOC EO and contingency plans.\n\n## 8.4 Deactivation and Transition\n\n22. Review the incident status, remaining threats, unresolved needs, response tasks, public information requirements and readiness of responsible offices.\n\n23. Obtain deactivation approval from the authority designated in the EOC EO. The EOC Manager shall not independently terminate an activation where approval is reserved to another official.\n\n24. Notify all EOC personnel, cluster desks, barangays and partner agencies of the decision, effective time and continuing coordination arrangements.\n\n25. Transfer outstanding tasks, resource requests, monitoring obligations, reporting duties and safety issues to named offices or the designated recovery/continuity mechanism.\n\n26. Close the operational period and ensure logbook, SitRep, decision, resource and communications records are complete, indexed and retained.\n\n27. Conduct a hotwash or after-action review as directed; record corrective actions and owners. Return the EOC to White only after the authorised deactivation and handover are completed.\n\n# 9. Incident Management and EOC–ICS Coordination\n\n## 9.1 Command and Coordination Boundary\n\nThe Incident Commander and IMT retain incident-level command and tactical control of field response under the applicable ICS arrangement. The EOC supports the Incident Commander and IMT through coordination, information management, resource coordination, inter-agency liaison and decision support. The EOC Manager does not issue field tactical orders unless separately designated by a valid command appointment within the ICS structure.\n\n**Table 4.11**\n\n*Command and Coordination Boundary Between the Incident Commander and the EOC*\n\n| **Function**              | **Incident Commander / IMT**                                                          | **EOC Manager / EOC**                                                                                      |\n|---------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Incident objectives       | Establishes and directs incident objectives and tactics under ICS.                    | Receives objectives, aligns coordination support and escalates policy/resource constraints.                |\n| Field operations          | Commands and supervises assigned field resources and operations.                      | Does not direct field teams; supports requested coordination and logistics.                                |\n| Information               | Provides verified operational updates and needs through designated channels.          | Consolidates, validates status and produces coordination products; preserves source and validation status. |\n| Resources                 | Identifies operational needs and submits/updates requests through the agreed process. | Coordinates sourcing, prioritisation, approvals and status visibility; tracks request to fulfilment.       |\n| Inter-agency coordination | Coordinates tactical operations at incident level as assigned.                        | Facilitates cross-office, cluster, utility, barangay and external liaison.                                 |\n| Executive decisions       | Recommends matters beyond incident authority through established escalation.          | Elevates decisions requiring Mayor/CDRRMC or another statutory authority.                                  |\n| Transition                | Recommends incident stabilisation, demobilisation and transfer of field tasks.        | Coordinates documentation, resource accountability and institutional handover.                             |\n\n**Figure 4.3**\n\n*Command and Coordination Boundary*\n\n![Figure 3: 9.1 Command and Coordination Boundary](/library/ch4v11/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Solid lines are reporting or command lines; dotted lines are coordination and support. The EOC supports the Incident Commander / IMT and does not command field operations; clusters and lead agencies act within their statutory mandates. CDRRMO sections as in the 2026 structure chart.\n\n## 9.2 Initial Incident Coordination\n\n28. Confirm incident identity, location, time, hazard, reporting source and verification status. Identify whether an Incident Commander or IMT has been designated.\n\n29. Confirm the incident command post, incident objectives, operational period and the preferred liaison/contact route, where provided by the approved ICS arrangements.\n\n30. Open the EOC operational record and establish an initial situation picture using source-labelled reports. Identify information gaps and the next update time.\n\n31. Confirm the affected barangays, population impact, evacuation, casualties/injuries where verified, infrastructure/service disruption, access constraints, environmental concerns and vulnerable groups, consistent with privacy rules.\n\n32. Identify urgent life-safety issues and unmet needs. Refer each to the responsible response agency/cluster and record acceptance, action status and escalation.\n\n33. Convene the relevant EOC sections and cluster/liaison desks. Confirm which office leads each function, its reporting route and expected output.\n\n34. Provide decision support to the Mayor/CDRRMC and Incident Commander/IMT through approved briefings and SitReps. Separate confirmed facts from estimates and unverified reports.\n\n## 9.3 Operational Period and Coordination Meetings\n\nThe EOC Manager shall organise coordination around the incident complexity and approved ICS/EOC procedure. An operational period is the defined time interval for which objectives, tasks and reporting arrangements apply; its duration shall be set by the Incident Commander/IMT and aligned with the approved EOC arrangement. The EOC shall not impose an arbitrary universal operational-period duration.\n\n**Table 4.12**\n\n*Operational Period Coordination Elements*\n\n| **Coordination element** | **Minimum content / record**                                                                                                                            |\n|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Briefing                 | Current situation, source and time, objectives, key risks, activated functions, safety, resources, decisions, communication and reporting requirements. |\n| Task assignment          | Task, lead office/agency, supporting actors, location, priority, due/update time, resource requirements and status.                                     |\n| Decision log             | Issue, options, evidence, decision-maker, decision, rationale, time, implementation owner and review trigger.                                           |\n| Situation update         | Changes since last report, confirmed impacts, estimates, information gaps, actions completed, outstanding needs and next update.                        |\n| Escalation               | Matter requiring executive/statutory decision, responsible authority, deadline/urgency, recommendation and evidence.                                    |\n| Shift handover           | Current objectives, incident status, risks, active requests, commitments, key contacts, open decisions and next reporting deadline.                     |\n\n# 10. Emergency Response and Protective Actions\n\n## 10.1 Response Priorities\n\nResponse priorities shall be set by the Incident Commander/IMT and competent authorities under the applicable law and approved plans. The EOC shall coordinate information and support to these priorities. The following order provides a common planning reference and does not displace incident-specific tactical judgment:\n\n35. Protect life and prevent imminent harm, including warning, evacuation, search and rescue, emergency medical care and urgent protection.\n\n36. Protect affected people and ensure safe, accessible and dignified evacuation, shelter/camp management, WASH, food, health, protection and psychosocial support through the responsible agencies.\n\n37. Protect critical facilities, lifelines, essential public services, responders and communities from cascading failures.\n\n38. Restore or maintain priority services and access, including power, water, telecommunications, roads, transport, health and other critical operations.\n\n39. Provide timely, accurate public information and manage information gaps, rumours and feedback.\n\n40. Prepare for stabilisation, demobilisation and transition to recovery, continuity and rehabilitation arrangements.\n\n## 10.2 Evacuation Coordination\n\nEvacuation decisions and orders shall be made by the authority designated in applicable law and the City''s approved ordinances, executive issuances and contingency plans. The CDRRMO and EOC coordinate technical information, recommendations, dissemination, support and monitoring; they do not create independent evacuation powers through this Manual.\n\n41. Assess the official warning/incident information, affected area, forecast/observed impact, lead time, access, at-risk population, facility suitability and remaining safe options.\n\n42. Prepare the recommendation or decision brief identifying geographic scope, target population, timing, means of transport, support for at-risk groups, destination facility and safeguards.\n\n43. Obtain the required approval/order from the competent authority. Record the authority, time, scope, basis and effective period.\n\n44. Disseminate the instruction using approved warning channels, barangay relays, accessible formats and direct coordination with institutions and facilities at risk.\n\n45. Coordinate transport, route management, evacuation centre opening and management, health/protection support, security, registration/counting arrangements and family information through the responsible agencies.\n\n46. Monitor implementation, access and facility capacity. Record household/person totals only in authorised records and aggregate operational summaries as permitted; do not put names of evacuees or patients in general logbook, public map or open SitRep fields.\n\n47. Review continuation, expansion, modification or termination through the competent authority. Coordinate safe return only when the relevant technical and local authority conditions are met.\n\n## 10.3 Search and Rescue, Medical and Public Safety Coordination\n\nSearch and rescue, emergency medical, fire, police, maritime and other specialist response functions shall be led by the agency or Incident Commander with the relevant statutory and professional mandate. The EOC coordinates requests, status, access, staging, logistics and cross-agency dependencies; it shall not direct tactical deployment or expose responders to unassessed hazards.\n\n- Confirm who is leading the incident/operation and the applicable command and safety arrangements before tasking support.\n\n- Ensure deployment information includes location, task, hazard exposure, communications, expected duration, equipment, medical/safety support and return/check-in arrangements.\n\n- Coordinate with hospitals, health facilities and the health cluster on receiving capacity, transport, referral and service continuity using authorised channels.\n\n- Protect sensitive medical and personal information. EOC-wide products shall carry only the operational details necessary for coordination and lawful reporting.\n\n- Record deployment requests, acceptance, mobilisation, status, completion and demobilisation. Escalate overdue check-ins, lost communications or safety incidents immediately.\n\n## 10.4 Shelter, Camp Coordination and Protection\n\nThe responsible social welfare, health, education, barangay and other sector offices shall lead their respective shelter, camp coordination and management (CCCM), protection and service functions under approved plans. The Brgy. Liloan site visit documented an established evacuation site, evacuation protocol and camp coordination/management arrangements; this is a local practice to be documented and assessed for transferability, not a basis to assume that all barangays have equivalent capacity.\n\n**Table 4.13**\n\n*Shelter, Camp Coordination and Protection Requirements*\n\n| **Operational requirement**      | **Minimum coordination output**                                                                                                                                              |\n|----------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Facility opening and suitability | Authorised opening record; verified safe-use status, capacity basis, accessibility, water/sanitation, fire safety and operating focal persons.                               |\n| Population and needs             | Aggregate counts and disaggregated needs where lawfully collected; clear status for “not reported” versus confirmed zero.                                                    |\n| Services                         | Responsible provider and schedule for health, WASH, food, protection, child protection, gender-based violence risk mitigation, MHPSS, education and other relevant services. |\n| Safety and accountability        | Complaint/feedback arrangements, safeguarding, privacy controls, referral route, security and incident escalation.                                                           |\n| Closure and return               | Competent authority decision, safety verification, return support, facility inspection, records and outstanding needs.                                                       |\n\nCamp coordination and camp management follow DSWD-DILG-DOH-DepEd Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2, s. 2021. The City organises a Camp Management Committee through the CDRRMC, with the City Social Welfare and Development Officer as overall coordinator and, as members, the Liga ng mga Barangay president, City Engineering, City Health, the Schools Division Superintendent, the Chief of Police and the NGO representative to the council. The CSWDO keeps the camp management plan with its resource map and the DAFAC registration of families. Form ER-F03 records only aggregates and service status; DAFAC and family data stay with the CSWDO under RA 10173.\n\n## 10.5 Public Information and Rumour Management\n\nPublic information shall be coordinated through the designated City spokesperson/public information arrangement. The EOC may compile verified facts and proposed messages but shall not publish unapproved policy positions, casualty figures, evacuation instructions or technical interpretations. Unverified reports shall be labelled as such and referred for verification.\n\n48. Receive the information/query/rumour and record channel, time, subject and affected area.\n\n49. Identify the competent source/owner to verify the claim. Check against official agency products, incident commander reports and validated sector information.\n\n50. Classify the item as confirmed, corrected, unverified, superseded or false based on evidence and authorised assessment.\n\n51. Prepare a clear holding statement or correction through the authorised public information route; retain the source and approval record.\n\n52. Monitor feedback and recurring questions, update the EOC situation picture and revise public information as necessary.\n\n# 11. Operational Records, Reporting and Data Protection\n\n## 11.1 Minimum Operational Record Set\n\n**Table 4.14**\n\n*Minimum Operational Record Set*\n\n| **Record**                       | **Minimum content**                                                                                                            | **Control**                                                                       |\n|----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Warning / monitoring log         | Source, product, issue and receipt time, hazard, area, validity, verification and escalation.                                  | Original source retained; update/supersession traceable.                          |\n| Activation record                | Trigger/reason, level, decision authority, approved time, activated functions, notification status and review/deactivation.    | Authority action; approval and server-confirmed record required.                  |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch4v1_v1.1.md (Chapter 4, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.6))','draft')
